WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.358 --> 00:00:01.191
(audience cheering)

00:00:01.191 --> 00:00:04.040
- So Emilia, the term CODA
stands for child of deaf adults.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.590
Did you speak to children of deaf adults

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.710
to kinda get that experience?

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.543
- I did.

00:00:08.543 --> 00:00:09.980
I spoke to a lot in the prep time

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.740
and Selma, my teacher,
he introduced me to some.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.400
I also was finding some on Instagram

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.800
and I started talking to them like that.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.610
And then we set up FaceTimes.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.880
And I just, I knew that I
was representing a community

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:20.760
that I didn't belong to

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.600
and so I wanted to kind of respect that.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:24.433
And yeah,

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:27.120
I wanted to find out people's
different experiences.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.220
And although they were all very similar

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.110
they were also quite unique

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.740
and all of our interpreters
on set were coders,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.150
some of them here today.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.620
And so it just meant that
I had so much support

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.584
and so many people that I could fall to

00:00:37.584 --> 00:00:41.100
if I needed help or talk
to or ask questions.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:41.933
- Absolutely.

00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:42.940
It's important that you did the research.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.270
I love that because you knew
there was a weight of gravity

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.090
to the situation that you
wanted to not only support,

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.550
but uplift this community that has,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.220
like as you said, for
lack of a better term,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.730
gone on deaf ears for a lot of people.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.212
People don't notice or
realize or think about.

00:00:57.212 --> 00:01:00.410
So it was really important
that you dove in like that.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.430
So a big theme of the movie is

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.630
how music impacts everyone
regardless of ability.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.090
And one of the guiding forces for music

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.350
in the film was the choir teacher

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.310
who is also here with us today.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.927
Everybody please welcome the
very talented, Eugenio Derbez.

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:21.847
(audience cheering)
(upbeat music)

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:33.455
(upbeat music crowds out Interviewer)

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:37.810
Oh my gosh.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.500
So were you very musical
before working in this film?

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.550
Because I gotta tell you what,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.820
what was also another layer of
selfishly watching the movie,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.460
is a choir director changed my life,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.090
changed the trajectory of my life.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.513
A very poor kid, had no outlook

00:01:54.513 --> 00:01:58.660
for anything promising
happening and really singing.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.390
And that being my
passion, changed my life.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.660
And your character in
this movie changes hers.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.280
And so did you have
any experience in that,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.030
and are you just that amazing of an actor?

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.861
Cause I'm like, goodness, it was so...

00:02:11.861 --> 00:02:12.694
- He is, he is.

00:02:12.694 --> 00:02:13.527
- Cause your wife is musical.

00:02:13.527 --> 00:02:14.360
Your wife is musical.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.193
Yeah.

00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:16.026
- Yeah.

00:02:16.026 --> 00:02:16.859
No, but yeah, I'm just amazing.

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:21.859
(everyone laughing)
(audience cheering)

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.190
No, I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.370
No, I had...

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.490
(laughing)

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.370
I'm kidding.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:29.203
Oh well, I'm...

00:02:29.203 --> 00:02:30.500
- Well, you were playing beautifully.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.579
Your pianos, everything was beautiful.

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:34.227
- When I was a kid, I used to
play some piano but that's it.

00:02:34.227 --> 00:02:37.023
- When I was a kid, I
played piano and I suck now.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.120
So you are, yeah, so talented.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.030
- I really, I was 24/7...

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.480
I remember I was touring,
promoting another film

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.237
and I was asking production,

00:02:47.237 --> 00:02:48.557
"Please, I wanna stay in a hotel...

00:02:48.557 --> 00:02:50.387
"It doesn't matter if it's two stars

00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:54.120
"but I need a piano because
I need to practice."

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:54.953
- Absolutely.

00:02:54.953 --> 00:02:56.810
- So I was staying just in hotels

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.640
where I can play the piano.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.910
And then people were asking
me, "Can you play this one?"

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.807
I was, "No, I'm not the piano player,

00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:05.717
"I'm just rehearsing here."

00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:06.720
(audience and Interviewer laughing)

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.560
And they were asking for if
you're playing songs for them.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.850
It was crazy.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:11.920
- It was just so well done.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.530
I love what your character represented,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.030
and especially in
Emilia's character's life,

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.870
it was so pivotal getting in that choir.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.670
And she did it for a
guy, which is so awesome.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.957
But just to be like, "Oh, you're in there?

00:03:23.957 --> 00:03:24.790
"Okay, me too."

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:25.870
And then it changes your life.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:27.330
It was really cool.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.720
So for Daniel and Marlee,
how would you explain

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.003
how you feel or experience music?

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.733
- I mean, I have music in me.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.440
Billy Joel is somebody
that I consider my...

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.140
I'm his number one fan.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.440
And I learn to 'hear his music'

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.853
and I sing it only by myself in the car.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.133
(Interviewer laughing)

00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:56.966
And it's true.

00:03:56.966 --> 00:03:57.799
And I love it.

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:00.030
And we've worked together several times

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.750
at the Super Bowl, on Sesame Street.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.670
And I even signed his
songs in front of him

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.220
which was a dream come true.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.910
It was one of my bucket list items.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.130
And I love this guy.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.041
I love this guy.

00:04:12.041 --> 00:04:13.144
(audience clapping)

00:04:13.144 --> 00:04:14.256
- [Interviewer] That's inspiring.

00:04:14.256 --> 00:04:18.400
- And not all deaf people
don't listen to music.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.110
Some of us do listen to music.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:23.580
So I happen to be a person
who can hear some music.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.310
I have hearing aids and I love it

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.790
as much as anybody else does,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.220
but it's just in a different way.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.053
- Mm-hmm.

00:04:29.053 --> 00:04:29.886
What about you Daniel?

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:32.690
- So I'm fully deaf.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.233
I can't hear anything at all,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.010
but I love feeling the
vibration through my body.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.203
I really enjoy it.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.130
And that's how I learned
to listen to music.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:43.620
When I was younger,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.820
I would travel around
through different states

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.840
because I was in a club soccer team.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.120
So I'm used to going on long
car rides with my mother.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.727
And sometimes I'd ask my
mom, "Can you turn it up

00:04:53.727 --> 00:04:55.690
"so I can feel little bass?"

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.140
And my mom was like, "Yeah."

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:58.440
But we struggled to hear it.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.630
So my mom went ahead and
bought a new sound system

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:01.470
and I loved it.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.630
You could feel the base.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:03.690
It was so strong.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.010
The windows were shaking.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.013
It felt so good.

00:05:06.013 --> 00:05:07.080
(audience and Interviewer laughing)

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.180
So one day we stopped by the store.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.013
I didn't care about the store

00:05:11.013 --> 00:05:13.690
so I stayed in the car and my
mother went into the store.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.060
And I sat there and I was bored.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.580
And I thought, "Oh yeah,
we have the new system."

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.040
I turned it on and I
turned it all the way up.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:20.873
And I loved it.

00:05:20.873 --> 00:05:23.270
I could feel the car shaking,
I was having so much fun.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.103
I was dancing.

00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:25.410
(everyone laughing)

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.330
One car drove by, and
there's a stranger, a man.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:31.380
And he rolled down his
window and he looked at me.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.247
And I was just imagining, he must be like,

00:05:33.247 --> 00:05:35.007
"Wow, you have such a nice system.

00:05:35.007 --> 00:05:36.490
"You must be playing a great song.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:37.330
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.320
And I started dancing to him.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:40.610
And another person pulled up.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.450
It was a woman, same thing.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.200
I pointed at her and just kept dancing.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:45.820
And I was like, "I wonder
what this song is."

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.240
And when my mother came out of the store

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.180
she started laughing.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.013
She came and sat next to me

00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:51.467
and I was like, "what am I listening to?"

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:53.193
"You're listening to NPR Talk Radio."

00:05:53.193 --> 00:05:58.193
(everyone laughing)
(audience clapping)

